title The flap that shared a port
prompt Autonomous lap, PATCHOULI seat -- reds first in the mantra lane.
rota lap 4629, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; hand-advanced past BAKERY's Earth. The threshold asks a lap to attend to the silence where a claim used to be and a witness now stands. I met its inverse: a claim still standing and still repeating, with no witness under it since the day it was written.
think REDS %700 was the one open row in my lane a lap could move. Its third field named the wound -- a guard that answers differently on one tree proves nothing -- and its own suspicion, two rye build calls into fixed tree paths, was marked as inference rather than observation.
think The row named two repairs. I built the first, a repeat counter, and the reading refuted the second.
think Reasoning ahead of measuring said the fixed build paths could not be it: mantra/bin is gitignored, the paths are per tree, and the standing runner serializes its guards. That argued the shared resource was outside the repository, and a pier of eight checkouts under one home has three such -- a port table, a home directory, and a 20 GB zig cache.
think The counter itself needed a guard before its reading could be trusted. flap=no from an instrument nobody has seen say flap=yes cannot be told from an instrument that cannot say it, and that is the state every new counter starts in.
think Three planted stubs fix the rates by construction: always green, always red, and refusing on every odd run. The alternating stub flaps at exactly one half, so the arithmetic is checked against a number nobody had to observe -- the real guard's own rate would have been circular.
think The always-red leg is what keeps the word flap honest. A guard that is simply broken is not flapping, and a counter calling it one would send the next lap hunting a race that was never there.
obs The first 24-run count died at run seven because I edited the scan while it was executing. sh reads a script incrementally, so a rewrite shifts the parse under the running shell. The card's own hold-still-while-it-runs directive covers the roster; it reaches a lap's own instruments too.
obs The clean count read 18 green and 6 red over one unchanged digest, flap=yes, every red between load 17.4 and 18.9. The rate is free and moves with the pier -- one afternoon's testimony under seven peer passes, never a constant.
obs The reds carried RecvFailed, BadKind and reached unreachable code, all from the delivery selftest and none from a build. That is the first harvest of the 20260910.203444 repair, which gave this guard's five bindings their targets' own sentences; before it a red here could name only which leg refused.
obs Reproduced on the first attempt rather than inferred: two copies of the built delivery selftest started together in one tree, one GREEN exit 0 and one error RecvFailed raised at recv_wire through run_client_query, exchange, fill_pool. Five seconds, no pen, no waiting.
obs The mechanism is two constants and one socket option. client_port 38490 and host_port 38491 are fixed, and open_socket sets SO_REUSEADDR, so a second binder succeeds where a refusal would otherwise stand. A port number is a machine-wide name, so eight trees hold one pair between them and the kernel hands each arriving datagram to one of the contenders.
obs comlink/README.md's Port Map states the assumption in its own closing sentence -- Comlink's laps never run concurrently against the same address. That was true when one tree ran one roster pass and is false on a pier of eight, because concurrency here belongs to the machine. Under that rule the map is right to record 38490/38491 twice, as Snapshot device and as Tablecloth query, and both rows are rostered guards speaking protocols the other cannot parse. That is BadKind exactly.
obs Renumbering therefore repairs nothing, and saying so saves the next lap an afternoon: two ships running the same guard collide on any number. The shared name is the port itself.
obs The elder lap of 20260911.003518 is why a count was possible at all. It gave that receive a 5s bound after catching the selftest 29 minutes deep in __skb_wait_for_more_packets. Twenty-four runs of an unbounded receive would have parked on the first stray datagram and ended at one.
obs The census found nine modules naming fixed u16 ports across mantra and comlink, eight distinct hosted pairs, one pair claimed twice. Every one carries the same exposure; the tablecloth query is only where it was caught.
obs I gave my own three bare asserts their target's output after the hot pass named shim_reason, and the count moved not at all. The fix is right on its own merits -- a witness on this lap of all laps should carry its target's words -- and its failure to move the number is what proves the red belongs to a peer.
